## Multi-Chip Package with Inter-Die Leadframes Forming Common Electrical Rails

### Abstract

A multi-chip semiconductor package includes a plurality of semiconductor dies arranged in a stacked, side-by-side, or partially overlapping configuration. One or more conductive leadframes are positioned between adjacent semiconductor dies. Each leadframe includes a plurality of electrically isolated leads associated with respective terminals of the adjacent dies. Corresponding leads of different leadframes are electrically and mechanically bonded together to define common electrical rails extending through the package. The common electrical rails may distribute power, ground, control, or signal connections among the semiconductor dies and to external package terminals. Locating the leadframes between the dies can reduce interconnect length, improve current-carrying capacity and thermal performance, and provide a modular package architecture suitable for power devices, integrated circuits, and heterogeneous semiconductor assemblies.

### Technical Field

The disclosure relates generally to semiconductor packaging and, more particularly, to multi-chip packages having conductive leadframes positioned between semiconductor dies and interconnected to form shared electrical rails.

### Overview

In one embodiment, a semiconductor package includes first and second semiconductor dies separated by an intermediate leadframe. The intermediate leadframe includes multiple conductive leads extending along or adjacent to active surfaces, terminal surfaces, or edge regions of the dies. Selected die terminals are bonded to respective leads by solder, conductive adhesive, sintered metal, thermocompression bonding, wire bonds, clips, ribbons, or another electrically conductive attachment structure.

The package may include multiple intermediate leadframes arranged in an alternating die-and-leadframe stack. Leads having corresponding electrical functions are joined across the leadframes. For example, respective positive-supply leads may be bonded together to form a common positive rail, respective ground or negative-supply leads may be bonded together to form a common return rail, and respective gate, control, sense, or communication leads may be bonded together to form additional common rails.

Bonding between corresponding leads may be provided at overlapping portions, edge portions, tabs, fingers, posts, vias, or other connection regions of the leadframes. The bonded leads collectively form a conductive bus extending longitudinally, laterally, or vertically through the package. At least one of the bonded leads may extend beyond a package body to define an external terminal, or may be connected to a ball, land, pin, pad, or other package-level interconnect.

### Example Package Structure

A representative package may include:

- a first semiconductor die having a first set of terminals;
- a second semiconductor die having a second set of terminals;
- a first conductive leadframe positioned between the first and second dies;
- optionally, one or more additional dies and leadframes arranged in an alternating stack;
- conductive attachments coupling die terminals to associated leadframe leads;
- bonded junctions coupling corresponding leads of the respective leadframes; and
- an encapsulant surrounding at least portions of the dies, leadframes, and bonded junctions.

The dies may be oriented with active surfaces facing in the same direction, in opposite directions, or toward an intervening leadframe. A leadframe may include conductive features on one or both sides to electrically contact terminals of adjacent dies. Dielectric films, spacers, coatings, or molded regions may electrically isolate non-corresponding leads and prevent unintended contact between the dies and leadframes.

### Common Electrical Rails

Each common rail may be formed from two or more physically separate leads that are joined during package assembly. The bonded leads may function as a unitary low-resistance conductor after bonding. In various implementations, the common rails include one or more of:

- a source, emitter, drain, or collector rail;
- a positive or negative power-supply rail;
- a ground or return rail;
- a gate or control rail;
- a common data or clock rail;
- a Kelvin-sense rail;
- a thermal-monitoring rail; or
- a shield or reference-potential rail.

The cross-sectional dimensions and number of bonded leads may be selected according to the expected current, voltage, impedance, and thermal requirements. High-current rails may include widened lead portions, multiple parallel bonds, conductive clips, or locally thickened regions.

### Manufacturing Method

An example method includes:

1. providing a plurality of semiconductor dies and conductive leadframes;
2. positioning at least one leadframe between adjacent dies;
3. aligning leads of the leadframes with corresponding electrical terminals or functional nodes;
4. attaching selected die terminals to associated leads;
5. bonding corresponding leads together to form one or more common electrical rails;
6. electrically isolating non-corresponding leads where required;
7. encapsulating at least part of the assembly; and
8. forming or exposing external package terminals coupled to the common rails.

The corresponding leads may be bonded before, during, or after placement of the dies. Bonding may be performed by welding, solder reflow, brazing, conductive adhesive curing, sintering, ultrasonic bonding, laser bonding, thermocompression bonding, or mechanical interlocking followed by metallurgical joining.

### Advantages and Applications

The inter-die leadframe arrangement can provide short, low-inductance current paths and can reduce reliance on long wire bonds or package-substrate routing. Bonded corresponding leads may distribute current among parallel dies and improve electrical symmetry. The leadframes may additionally function as heat spreaders, structural supports, die-alignment features, or electromagnetic shields.

The architecture may be used with power MOSFETs, insulated-gate bipolar transistors, diodes, thyristors, analog devices, logic devices, memory devices, sensors, radio-frequency devices, or combinations thereof. Example applications include power modules, voltage regulators, motor-control assemblies, battery-management systems, converters, inverters, and heterogeneous system-in-package devices.

### Illustrative Independent Claim

1. **A semiconductor package comprising:**  
   a plurality of semiconductor dies;  
   a plurality of conductive leadframes, at least one of the conductive leadframes being positioned between adjacent semiconductor dies of the plurality of semiconductor dies, each conductive leadframe comprising a plurality of leads;  
   conductive connections coupling terminals of the semiconductor dies to respective ones of the leads; and  
   a plurality of bonded junctions, each bonded junction electrically coupling corresponding leads of at least two of the conductive leadframes, wherein the corresponding leads and the bonded junction collectively define a common electrical rail shared by at least two of the semiconductor dies.

### Illustrative Method Claim

2. **A method of forming a semiconductor package, the method comprising:**  
   arranging a plurality of semiconductor dies with respective conductive leadframes positioned between adjacent semiconductor dies;  
   electrically coupling terminals of the semiconductor dies to selected leads of the conductive leadframes; and  
   bonding corresponding leads of different conductive leadframes together to form at least one common electrical rail extending through the semiconductor package.
